{"caller": "codex", "commit": "e9f07fe90041cb184966733876b4cccc50e58dcd", "date": "2026-10-06T14:51:14+00:00", "exit": 0, "files": ["SqliteVerifier/Declarations.lean", "plans/20261006-checked-api-reference.status.md", "reviews/log.jsonl", "tests/test_checked_public_docs.py"], "findings": [{"id": "20261006T145114Z-e9f07fe9#1", "line": 25, "path": "SqliteVerifier/Declarations.lean", "rule": "R9", "severity": "should", "text": "The new `DeclaredType` docstring no longer says why the model keeps the type spelling apart from the affinity; the old text gave that reason, which is the rowid trap (a BIGINT primary key does not alias the rowid, but INTEGER does). Fix: say that the model keeps the spelling because it decides whether a primary-key column aliases the rowid."}, {"id": "20261006T145114Z-e9f07fe9#2", "line": 102, "path": "SqliteVerifier/Declarations.lean", "rule": "R9", "severity": "should", "text": "The new `Column.plain` docstring says what the test checks but not why: `Execution.lean` uses it to admit only plain columns in CREATE TABLE and ADD COLUMN. Fix: say that the supported subset admits only plain columns in created and added columns, so the model does not need NOT NULL, defaults or alias spellings there."}], "id": "20261006T145114Z-e9f07fe9", "kind": "review", "reviewer": "claude", "revision": "@-", "rules": {"R1": "62d446279093", "R10": "638d8cbd1df8", "R11": "5de19cdbbbc1", "R12": "99f66579f467", "R13": "da0926dafb88", "R2": "e5e78fb2a52a", "R3": "4f9188eadd60", "R4": "ea7ca17539eb", "R5": "b8613a2d693b", "R6": "5600dce59aef", "R7": "0b68df581c57", "R8": "b4168359c60d", "R9": "5a69196344d0"}, "well_formed": true}
